Chapter 5: Exploring the Relationship of Socioeconomic Indicators with Fertility Rate
Scatter plots with regression (trend) lines are used to explore the relationship between each socioeconomic indicator and the fertility rate.
Fertility Rates and Agriculture Levels

Fig. 12: Fertility vs Agriculture

Relationship: Positive correlation — fertility rates are historically higher in agricultural societies due to the economic benefits of larger families.
Historical Context:
In traditional agricultural societies like rural France in the 19th and early 20th centuries, families had more children to provide labor for farming. In contrast, Swiss regions that industrialized earlier saw smaller family sizes as urban jobs required fewer workers and higher living costs reduced the economic incentive for large families.
Modern Context: In rural areas with a stronger agricultural base, fertility rates may still be slightly higher, though mechanization has reduced reliance on manual labor.

Fertility Rates and Education Attainment

Fig. 13: Fertility vs Education

Relationship: Negative correlation — higher education levels, particularly among women, are associated with lower fertility rates.
Historical Context:
In France, highly educated regions like Île-de-France historically reported lower fertility compared to less urbanized areas. Similarly, in Switzerland, rising education in the 20th century was accompanied by declining fertility rates as women entered the workforce (UNESCO, 2023).

Fertility Rates and Examination Levels

Fig. 14: Fertility vs Examination

Relationship: Negative correlation — examination-driven education systems emphasize career development over family formation.
Historical Context:
In Switzerland, rigorous vocational and academic pathways encourage students to focus on career development, delaying family formation. France's centralized system (the baccalauréat) similarly incentivizes extended education.

Fertility Rates and Catholic Beliefs

Fig. 15: Fertility vs Catholics

Relationship: Positive correlation — Catholicism traditionally encourages larger families and opposes contraception.
Historical Context:
In France, historically Catholic regions like Alsace and Lorraine have exhibited higher fertility compared to secular regions like Paris. In Switzerland, Catholic-majority cantons (e.g., Valais) had higher fertility than Protestant-majority ones (e.g., Zurich).
Modern Context: The influence of Catholicism on fertility has diminished due to secularization and the widespread acceptance of contraception.

Fertility Rates and Infant Mortality Rates

Fig. 16: Fertility vs Infant Mortality

Relationship: Positive correlation — as infant mortality decreases, families tend to have fewer children.
Historical Context:
In 19th-century France and Switzerland, high infant mortality led to larger families as insurance against child loss. As healthcare improved in the 20th century, fertility rates declined. Switzerland's advanced healthcare is reflected in one of the world's lowest infant mortality rates and correspondingly low fertility.

