Conclusion
This assignment demonstrated how descriptive and inferential statistics are essential tools for examining socioeconomic data to support informed decision-making. By analysing the Swiss dataset — covering 47 municipalities across French and Swiss regions — we identified key relationships between fertility rates and socioeconomic factors.
Key findings:

Higher education levels and lower infant mortality rates are associated with lower fertility rates.
Higher agricultural involvement and greater Catholic population share are associated with higher fertility rates.

Descriptive statistics provided a clear picture of data distributions and central tendencies, while inferential methods (regression analysis) enabled exploration of relationships between variables.
These findings highlight the value of evidence-based policymaking for tackling demographic challenges (Pew Research Center, 2023). By leveraging statistical tools, policymakers can better understand population trends and implement targeted interventions to support sustainable development.

